
<p>CNBC:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>But Trump’s jubilation stands in stark contrast to the reaction
from even his staunchest allies on Capitol Hill, who recoiled
after the reinstatement and vowed to continue their investigations
into Huang — whose relationship with a potential Chinese
intelligence asset they fear may have jeopardized U.S. national
security.</p>

<p>“The risk remains that there could be information that a Chinese
spy knows about a key individual in the supply chain, and that’s
not worth the risk,” Sen. Kevin Cramer, R-N.D., said Tuesday on
CNBC’s “Squawk Box.” “We might even have to put a babysitter on
Jensen Huang for crying out loud ... just to make sure that he
doesn’t do something that’s going to risk the United States.”</p>
</blockquote>

<p>What’s conspicuous isn’t merely that <em>some</em> congressional Republicans are denouncing Trump’s actions on behalf of Huang (whether on <a href="https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/2026/11/trump-huang-national-security-risk/689547/">the specific national security issues</a> related to Nvidia and China, or <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2026/11/24/nvidia-and-trumps-state-capitalism">the general abandonment</a> of the pre-Trump bedrock Republican principle of laissez-faire capitalism) — it’s that none, seemingly, are defending it. <a href="https://www.washingtonsun.com/congress/nvidia-jensen-huang-reinstatement-questioning">Here’s a report from The Washington Sun</a> (formerly NOTUS) on Monday quoting a host of Republicans expressing some degree of opposition to Trump’s actions.</p>

<p><a href="https://talkingpointsmemo.com/edblog/day-two-isnt-looking-great-for-trump-or-nvidia/sharetoken/8799ef26-4251-47da-809c-f7bdfcc55e4d">Josh Marshall at TPM</a> (gift link):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Over the last 24 hours various Silicon Valley types have been
thumping their chests over Jensen Huang’s return, lecturing
Democrats about Huang’s irreplaceability and so forth. But the
reality is that none of this has anything to do with Democrats.
Trump’s move yesterday is <a href="https://x.com/davidshor/status/2125254888905224579">wildly unpopular</a>, and the tech
oligarchs are probably the most unpopular powerful and
non-imprisoned people in the country today. For anyone who has
their eyes open, Trump is not only unpopular but rapidly losing
power, even among fellow Republicans, in a way that would have
been unimaginable even a few months ago when numerous Senate
Republicans came out publicly yesterday attacking Trump’s effort
to shield Huang in the ongoing chip-smuggling and spy
investigation. They’re all clustering together as their power ebbs
and isolation grows.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The stock market <a href="https://www.techmeme.com/261125/p24#a261125p24">seems to have lost much of its enthusiasm</a> for this intervention as well.</p>

